<article>
  <div class="container article-body" style="max-width:880px;margin:0 auto;">
    <img src="/Images/dilly-bar.webp" alt="Dairy Queen Classic Chocolate Coated Dilly Bar on stick — a 210-calorie portion-controlled frozen treat" width="880" height="480" loading="eager" style="width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:24px;margin:32px 0;box-shadow: 0 8px 30px rgba(11,30,56,0.1);border:1px solid #EDE4D6;">
    
    <div class="article-body">
      <p class="lead">Fast-food dessert chains are routinely portrayed as dietary danger zones. When a Large Brownie Blizzard exceeds 1,300 calories and a Triple FlameThrower Stackburger tops 1,150 calories with a day's worth of sodium, critics assume healthy eating at Dairy Queen is an oxymoron. In reality, with a firm grasp of macronutrient ratios, portion geometry, and kitchen modifiers, DQ can comfortably fit into strict athletic diets, bodybuilding cutting phases, and diabetic glycemic plans. Here is our <strong>2026 Nutritional Laboratory Field Guide</strong> to hacking the DQ menu for maximum protein and minimum caloric drag.</p>

      <h2>The Protein-to-Calorie (P:C) Efficiency Hierarchy</h2>
      <p>When tracking macros, what matters most is protein density: how many grams of lean amino acids you receive per 100 calories of food consumed. Our research desk audited the entire hot-food lineup to construct the 2026 Protein Efficiency Index:</p>

      <div class="table-wrap">
